+
+## XML checkpoint integration
+
+The native trainer now writes `adapter.xml` through DayCare's shared artifact
+writer with a **lossless** storage policy derived from each tensor dtype and verifies byte-exact reload before
+recording completion. XML stores the base-model hash, rank/alpha, exact tensor
+destinations, training settings and input/code provenance. Evaluation is marked
+pending; completing optimization never implies passing the external task gate.
+`run.json` remains the detailed loss/provenance ledger and declares
+`adapter_file` and `adapter_format`. Dataset/envelope input remains JSON, and
+feature caches remain NPZ; this is XML adapter integration, not a claim that
+every internal file is XML.
+
+Continuation accepts XML and legacy NPZ. XML additionally validates the base
+hash, rank, alpha and destination map before assigning any tensors. The Nemotron
+merge/export consumer follows the recorded adapter filename, accepts both
+formats, and verifies its hash. Fractional alpha is preserved by XML parsing.
+
+The original implementation introduced in `4cc927e` wrote NPZ directly and
+bypassed the existing XML writer. No documented rationale was found. Historical
+experiments therefore remain NPZ experiments. To create an explicitly later,
+lossless XML copy without modifying the historical directory:
+
+```sh
+python3 scripts/convert_native_lora_xml.py --run /path/to/original/train \
+  --output /path/to/new-xml-conversion
+```
+
+The converter refuses an existing destination, checks the original adapter hash,
+and records both source hashes and byte-equality proof. It performs no training.
+The real `calculator-verified-sft-001` adapter was converted into a separate
+runtime directory; all four float32 tensors round-trip exactly. Its historical
+NPZ SHA-256 is `ed8ee92b7c9405f4838c353fce4accc553abbd226459acbe5dc105d1a3b07194`.
+This proves serialization parity, not an accuracy improvement or an adoption
+gate. A CPU fixture also executes optimization, XML save, reload, and continued
+optimization through the native runner.
+
+Authority review: `daycare.artifact.native_lora` owns checkpoint compatibility;
+the existing XML codec/manifest owns serialization. Training and the historical
+Nemotron exporter are callers. The shared codec now owns dtype/storage selection as well as tensor encoding.
+The general decoder backend and inference harness are unchanged. This addition crosses the 18,000-line product review
+threshold, remains under the 20,000-line hard budget, and avoids
+duplicating the existing XML serializer.
+
+Storage type authority is `daycare.artifact.codec`: its registry defines
+supported dtypes, codecs and GGUF tensor IDs. Both XML adapters and model
+packages resolve storage there before any cast. Lossless selection currently
+supports float16 and float32 and rejects unsupported types (including float64
+and unregistered BF16) rather than silently narrowing. Explicit lossy policies
+remain available. Native checkpoints request the policy, not a specific quant.
+The XML records each tensor's concrete derived format; decoding continues to
+return the artifact layer's float32 compute representation.
